MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The QC Rejection Analysis listed every fault QC recorded on a batch's rejected
slabs — Crack, Pinhole, Oil Dot, Chipout and the rest — which read on screen as
"the Robo line caused these". Only two QC faults are the Robo line's: Spillage
and Pattern Problem in QC Line. Show and count those, nothing else.

- qcRejection: define the two Robo-line faults and recognise QC's free-text
  spellings of them by exact normalised text (case, spacing, hyphens, trailing
  punctuation) — never a substring, so Pattern Variation / pattern Blur stay
  out; "pattern problem" is accepted as QC's master spelling of Pattern Problem
  in QC Line. Only C (Reject) slabs count, each once however many Robo faults
  it carries. Rejection Rate is now Robo-line rejects / Total Slabs Produced
  x 100 (owner's formula: 20 / 250 = 8%). The table always shows exactly the
  two faults in a fixed order, with 0 when one did not occur; % is of the
  Robo-line rejects. The non-Robo figures (all-fault reject total, downgrades,
  rejects with no fault) are no longer computed or returned.
- DownloadsClient: the four KPIs are small cards — Total Slabs Produced, QC
  Inspected, "Total Rejected Slabs due to robots/robo line", "Rejection Rate";
  the table lists only the two Robo-line faults; the section says it is
  Robo-line only; the in-progress-QC note is reworded for the new denominator.
- route: doc only — the response now carries Robo-line figures alone.
- tests: rewritten for the new rules (other faults excluded, near-miss pattern
  faults excluded, spelling variants, the 250/20 = 8% example, overlap counted
  once, B not a rejection, zero and fixed-order cases).

