From cf20fcc64e20955054fd3346a87c735b7a7f07c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 06:20:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] robo(reports): Production Rate per Hour and its KPIs draw
 a slab where it was made, not a day off
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Batch D-1432 ran on 31 Aug only, 11:20 -> 22:31, but the chart drew two of its
last-hour slabs on 1 Sep (21:00-22:00 -> 1, 22:00-23:00 -> 1), and the same
placement read Total Production Time 34 hours 40 minutes and Avg Slabs/hour
3.6. The owner saw the same in 1426, 1411, 1410, 1404 and 1384.

Root cause: the placement trusted each slab's stored date absolutely, and the
records carry two recording slips that both land a slab EXACTLY one day away
from where it was made:
  1. a production date one day off - the entry form's working date carries
     forward (re-seeded from the setup's date, moved on by hand at midnight),
     so slabs keyed before the operator notices keep the wrong day while their
     In/Out clock times are right. Real exports show both directions: D-1448's
     160534-160535 (made 18 Sep 00:22-00:46) are stored 17 Sep; D-1449's
     161281 is stored 21 Sep between slabs made 22 Sep 07:40 and 07:51;
  2. an Out typed a few minutes before its own In, which the midnight rule
     (Out < In = crossed midnight) puts on the next day.

- hourlyProduction: placeSlabs is now the one placement for the chart, its
  subtitle and the KPIs. Each slab stays on its stored date unless, in
  production (physical slab-number) order, it has little company where it was
  placed and a clear majority of its neighbours sit exactly one day away,
  right beside its clock time - then the slab (1) or just its rolled Out (2)
  is placed on that day. Judged against neighbours' STORED places, at most one
  day per slab, no day cursor (so none of the old serialNumber walk's drift),
  kept only if the run gets no less consistent with production order, and
  never on runs under five slabs. Nothing is written back: Slab Records, Date
  Wise and the exports still show the stored date. runBounds gives the run's
  first In / last Out; runScope formats them for the subtitle. The chart's
  length cap goes from four to eight days: D-1449 really ran 17 -> 23 Sep and
  the four-day cap cut its chart off on the 21st.
- productionSpan: Total Production Time is runBounds (same placement as the
  chart), so Avg Slabs/hour follows the corrected span. Removed stampMinutes,
  unused since the Reference Sheet change.
- reportSummary: passes each slab's slab number, entry time and id, and its
  run - the whole batch when Reports is filtered to one (exactly the set the
  chart draws), otherwise its batch number.
- hourly route: returns the run's first In and last Out with the series.
- ReportsClient: chart subtitle now reads e.g. "Batch D-1432 · 31/08/2026
  (11:20) -> 31/08/2026 (22:31) - slabs completed each hour, by Out Time, across
  the batch's run", from the first slab's In and the last slab's Out.
- tests: D-1432 rebuilt from the Reports screen (reproduces 34h 40m / 3.6 and
  the 1 Sep points as stored; after: one date, 21:00 -> 14, 22:00 -> 5,
  11 hours 11 minutes, 11.3) for both slips; the real D-1448 and D-1449
  exports as fixtures (exactly 2 and 1 slabs move; D-1449's four pauses of
  6-60 h and seven midnight crossings untouched); 1426-shaped multi-day runs
  with late tails; head slips; real midnight crossings, long holds and a real
  next-day restart left alone; row order irrelevant; subtitle text; source
  guards on the wiring; and property checks over 1,000 generated runs (no
  move on clean records, isolated slips always recovered and stable, never
  more than one day, nothing lost). Mutation-checked.

