From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 08:22:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] =?UTF-8?q?robo(reports):=20Daily=20Slabs=20/=20Hour?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20Trend=20=3D=20slabs=20on=20the=20date=20=C3=B7=20hours=20the?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20line=20ran=20that=20date?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Trend Analysis rate read 19/09 as 279.3 slabs/hour. It divided each
date's slabs by the open time of the RoboShift rows attributed to that date -
rows the entry form creates silently when the tablet is first used on a day
and closes when the next day's first slab is saved, with an open one measured
to the wall clock. Their start and end are tablet times, not production
times, so a date whose shift row spanned a quarter of an hour gave a rate in
the hundreds.

The rate is now date-based, from the production records alone:

  Daily Slabs/hour = slabs whose Production Date is that date
                     / hours the Robo line ran on that date

- dailyRate (new, pure): the hours come from every slab's In and Out. Between
  two such moments the line counts as running when they are at most two
  hours apart - delays, changeovers and slabs held in the line are not
  subtracted (the owner's rule); a longer stretch with nothing going in or
  out is the line stopped (e.g. the 5h 36m power cut on 22 Sep, the line off
  overnight) and is not counted. Stretches are split at midnight, so a day
  that ran throughout divides by 24 and one that ran 07:00 -> 20:00 by 13.
  Moments rather than In->Out intervals, so an Out typed before its In, or a
  slab left in the line through a stop, cannot paint an idle day as
  production. The chart's Y-axis is 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, extended in 2s only
  if a date really tops 10.
- trends route: slabsPerHour from dailyRate, plus lineMinutes (the divisor)
  for the tooltip; the shift-row timing is gone. The day before the window is
  read for its after-midnight running time only. slabs and delayMins - the
  Daily Production Trend and Day-wise Delay Analysis - are counted exactly as
  before.
- SlabsPerHourLine: fixed 0-10 axis; the tooltip names the slabs and running
  time behind each value ("7.1 slabs/hr (110 slabs in 15h 30m)"); the card's
  subtitle says what the rate is.
- tests: the owner's examples (a full day / 24, 110 slabs over 07:00-20:00 =
  8.5), delays kept, long stops dropped, the two-hour boundary, midnight,
  the eve's overnight slabs, Out-before-In and held slabs, untimed slabs, the
  axis, the real D-1448/D-1449 registers (every date 6-10 slabs/hour; 20 Sep
  193 / 24 h), and source guards that the other two series are unchanged.
  Mutation-checked.

